{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the whole congregation of the children of Israel murmured against Moses</b> — It would have been fitting for Scripture to state at the beginning the substance of their complaint, as it says at Marah (Exodus 15:24) and at Rephidim (Exodus 17:2) and in all the complaints, and to say here, \"and there is no flesh to eat and no bread to satisfy,\" \"and the people hungered for bread.\" Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 16:1) that Scripture stated <b>on the fifteenth day of the second month</b> in order to single out this encampment, because on that very day the matzah-dough which they had brought out of Egypt was exhausted and they came to need the manna, to teach you that they ate from the remainder of the dough for sixty-one meals. And this is the received tradition of our Sages (Mekhilta here). Therefore he did not specify their complaints, because Scripture did not elaborate on this miracle that was performed for them in secret, and I have already written the reason for this in the portion of <i>Vayyigash elav</i> (Genesis 46:15). And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 16:1) that Scripture mentioned <b>on the fifteenth day of the second month</b> to say that already a full month of days had passed since they left Egypt, and they had eaten the bread which they brought out of Egypt and their livestock — for they were a great multitude — and this is the reason for the complaint.\n\nIn my opinion, Scripture mentioned <b>and they came to the wilderness of Sin</b> to make known their complaint: for when they came to the wilderness of Sin and had already traveled far from Egypt, they said, \"What shall we eat, and what will sustain us in the great wilderness through which we are journeying?\" — or because at first they supposed they would come to the cities in the surrounding region after a few days, and now a month had passed and they found no settled city, and they said, \"We shall all die in this great wilderness into which we have come.\" And this is the meaning of <b>and they murmured against Moses and against Aaron in the wilderness</b> — for their complaint was because of the wilderness. And so they said (Exodus 16:3), <b>for you have brought us out into this wilderness to put to death this whole assembly with hunger</b> — they mentioned the wilderness and the assembly, to say that so great an assembly would surely die of hunger in this great wilderness. And the Holy One, blessed be He, heard them, and now began to set a table for them in the wilderness until they would come to an inhabited land."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:02.127133486Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}